1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,626
Hello!

2
00:00:00,676 --> 00:00:05,570
This is the demo of Group 2's capstone project for the Knowledge Graphs course.

3
00:00:06,733 --> 00:00:10,210
We built a knowledge graph of 250 films from TMDB data,

4
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:15,532
and we went through all five steps of the assignment: an ontology, data collection,

5
00:00:15,582 --> 00:00:20,533
conversion to four-star RDF, links to Wikidata and DBpedia for the fifth star,

6
00:00:20,583 --> 00:00:22,136
and querying with SPARQL.

7
00:00:22,186 --> 00:00:27,457
In this video we show three parts: the ontology with the reasoner, the linked data,

8
00:00:27,507 --> 00:00:28,483
and the queries.

9
00:00:29,633 --> 00:00:31,746
This is our ontology in Protégé.

10
00:00:31,796 --> 00:00:36,612
We reuse existing vocabularies: Movie from schema.org, Person from FOAF,

11
00:00:36,662 --> 00:00:41,410
director and starring from DBpedia, and we only define what is missing,

12
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:43,168
like hasGenre or CastRole.

13
00:00:43,218 --> 00:00:45,195
Look at the class ActionMovie.

14
00:00:45,245 --> 00:00:49,250
It is defined as an equivalent class: hasGenre value Action.

15
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:53,643
In other words, any film whose genre is Action is an ActionMovie.

16
00:00:53,693 --> 00:00:58,847
Because it is an equivalence, the condition is both necessary and sufficient,

17
00:00:58,897 --> 00:01:01,821
so the machine can classify films by itself.

18
00:01:01,871 --> 00:01:05,943
The yellow rows below are members that the reasoner inferred.

19
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:08,707
We start the HermiT reasoner.

20
00:01:08,757 --> 00:01:13,050
On the full dataset this takes about three minutes, so we have cut the wait.

21
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:19,317
Then we ask the DL Query tab: which individuals belong to ActionMovie?

22
00:01:19,367 --> 00:01:21,015
Protégé lists 87 names.

23
00:01:21,065 --> 00:01:24,115
That is 29 films, each listed three times,

24
00:01:24,165 --> 00:01:30,094
because owl:sameAs makes our film the same individual as its Wikidata and DBpedia

25
00:01:30,144 --> 00:01:30,685
entries.

26
00:01:30,735 --> 00:01:34,819
Not a single film was tagged as an action movie by hand.

27
00:01:34,869 --> 00:01:37,550
They are all inferred from the genre.

28
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:43,431
In the same way, we get 16 comedies and 32 Oscar-winning directors,

29
00:01:43,481 --> 00:01:46,000
with the awards taken from Wikidata.

30
00:01:46,050 --> 00:01:51,352
People who direct and star in the same film cannot be described with an OWL

31
00:01:51,402 --> 00:01:53,850
restriction, so we add a SWRL rule.

32
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,176
After the reasoner runs again, five people appear: Clint Eastwood, Kevin Costner,

33
00:02:01,226 --> 00:02:06,710
Mel Gibson, Terry Gilliam and Woody Allen, each again under three names.

34
00:02:07,867 --> 00:02:15,657
Our check_reasoning script gives exactly the same numbers: 29, 16, 32, and 5.

35
00:02:16,833 --> 00:02:21,457
Here is the film The Prestige in our RDF data: its director,

36
00:02:21,507 --> 00:02:27,687
three actors with their cast roles, the genre, the studio, and the release year.

37
00:02:27,737 --> 00:02:34,152
The last two lines are what matter: owl:sameAs links this film to its DBpedia page,

38
00:02:34,202 --> 00:02:36,567
and to item Q46551 on Wikidata.

39
00:02:36,617 --> 00:02:38,358
That is the fifth star.

40
00:02:38,408 --> 00:02:42,174
We have 920 links to Wikidata and 912 to DBpedia.

41
00:02:42,224 --> 00:02:47,471
We match on the TMDB id that Wikidata already stores, not on titles,

42
00:02:47,521 --> 00:02:51,287
because matching by title and year found nothing.

43
00:02:51,337 --> 00:02:56,895
We also checked 30 links by hand: Wikidata was right 30 times out of 30,

44
00:02:56,945 --> 00:02:58,063
and DBpedia 29.

45
00:02:59,233 --> 00:03:04,004
Our query interface is a terminal tool, plus the SPARQL tab in Protégé.

46
00:03:04,054 --> 00:03:09,436
Query 3 answers which director has the most films in our data: Steven Spielberg,

47
00:03:09,486 --> 00:03:10,183
with eight.

48
00:03:11,333 --> 00:03:13,831
Query 16 uses our cast roles, an n-ary relation:

49
00:03:13,881 --> 00:03:16,697
each appearance of an actor in a film is its own node,

50
00:03:16,747 --> 00:03:19,510
so we can record the character and the billing order.

51
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:22,376
For The Dark Knight, Christian Bale plays Bruce Wayne,

52
00:03:22,426 --> 00:03:24,128
and Heath Ledger plays the Joker.

53
00:03:24,178 --> 00:03:27,843
A plain starring link from film to actor cannot hold that information.

54
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,226
The best part is the federated query.

55
00:03:31,276 --> 00:03:33,809
Our data has no birth dates for directors.

56
00:03:33,859 --> 00:03:38,913
This query follows Christopher Nolan's sameAs link and uses SERVICE to ask Wikidata

57
00:03:38,963 --> 00:03:42,542
directly, then joins the answer with his films in our data.

58
00:03:42,592 --> 00:03:46,540
Result: Nolan was born on the thirtieth of July, 1970, in London,

59
00:03:46,590 --> 00:03:50,107
and we list his seven films, from Memento to Interstellar.

60
00:03:50,157 --> 00:03:55,150
Thanks to the five-star links, one query enriches our data from an outside source.

61
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:59,043
Finally, the reasoner also catches mistakes.

62
00:03:59,093 --> 00:04:01,837
We load one film that is deliberately wrong:

63
00:04:01,887 --> 00:04:04,630
it has both the Action and the Comedy genre,

64
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:08,185
while ActionMovie and ComedyMovie are declared disjoint.

65
00:04:08,235 --> 00:04:13,010
We start the reasoner, and HermiT reports that the ontology is inconsistent.

66
00:04:14,167 --> 00:04:18,395
The Explain button shows exactly why: the film falls into both classes,

67
00:04:18,445 --> 00:04:20,383
and the two classes are disjoint.

68
00:04:20,433 --> 00:04:24,058
It is the same pattern as Russell's paradox from the lecture,

69
00:04:24,108 --> 00:04:27,252
and it is why disjointness must be stated explicitly.

70
00:04:27,302 --> 00:04:29,180
Under the open world assumption,

71
00:04:29,230 --> 00:04:33,277
the Semantic Web would not treat this as a contradiction on its own.

72
00:04:34,433 --> 00:04:39,758
To sum up, we went through all five steps: ontology, data, RDF, links and SPARQL,

73
00:04:39,808 --> 00:04:42,213
plus reasoning and federated queries.

74
00:04:42,263 --> 00:04:46,128
The current limits are that our URIs still use example.org,

75
00:04:46,178 --> 00:04:50,906
so they cannot be opened on the web, and each film keeps only one genre.

76
00:04:50,956 --> 00:04:52,896
All the code is on our GitHub.

77
00:04:52,946 --> 00:04:54,223
Thanks for watching!
